{"id":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch03_Curvature#def:pet-ch3-directional-curvature-operator","title":"def:pet-ch3-directional-curvature-operator","path":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch03_Curvature.md","line":113,"body":"","kind":"definition","status":"not_started","tex":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch03_Curvature.tex#def:pet-ch3-directional-curvature-operator","uses":["[[references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch03_Curvature#def:pet-ch3-curvature-tensor]]","[[references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch03_Curvature#prop:pet-ch3-curvature-symmetries]]"],"inspired_by":[],"bindings":[],"is_index":false,"body_html":"","formal":[],"tex_content":{"path":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch03_Curvature.tex","label":"def:pet-ch3-directional-curvature-operator","line":218,"source":"\\begin{definition}[directional curvature operator]\n  \\label{def:pet-ch3-directional-curvature-operator}\n\n  \\source{petersen:page-0101}\n  \\uses{def:pet-ch3-curvature-tensor, prop:pet-ch3-curvature-symmetries}\n\n  For $v\\in T_pM$, the \\emph{directional} (or \\emph{tidal force}) \\emph{curvature\n  operator} is $R_v(w)=R(w,v)v:T_pM\\to T_pM$. It is self-adjoint and $v$ is always\n  a zero eigenvector, by the symmetry properties of $R$.\n\\end{definition}","html":" <p></p><section class=\"theorem\"><div class=\"theorem-label\">Definition</div></section>[directional curvature operator] <span class=\"tex-label\" id=\"tex-label-6465663a7065742d6368332d646972656374696f6e616c2d6375727661747572652d6f70657261746f72\"></span> <p></p><span class=\"tex-source\" style=\"display:block\">Source: <span class=\"reference\">petersen:page-0101</span></span> <a class=\"wikilink tex-use\" href=\"#node=references%2FPetersen%2Fblueprint%2Fsrc%2Fchapters%2FCh03_Curvature%23def%3Apet-ch3-curvature-tensor\">↗ def:pet-ch3-curvature-tensor</a> <a class=\"wikilink tex-use\" href=\"#node=references%2FPetersen%2Fblueprint%2Fsrc%2Fchapters%2FCh03_Curvature%23prop%3Apet-ch3-curvature-symmetries\">↗ prop:pet-ch3-curvature-symmetries</a> <p></p>For <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$v\\in T_pM$\">$v\\in T_pM$</span>, the <em>directional</em> (or <em>tidal force</em>) <em>curvature operator</em> is <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$R_v(w)=R(w,v)v:T_pM\\to T_pM$\">$R_v(w)=R(w,v)v:T_pM\\to T_pM$</span>. It is self-adjoint and <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$v$\">$v$</span> is always a zero eigenvector, by the symmetry properties of <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$R$\">$R$</span>. ","warnings":[]},"previous":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch03_Curvature#def:pet-ch3-curvature-operator","next":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch03_Curvature#def:pet-ch3-sectional-curvature","relations":[{"kind":"uses","id":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch03_Curvature#def:pet-ch3-curvature-tensor","title":"def:pet-ch3-curvature-tensor","label":"def:pet-ch3-curvature-tensor"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch03_Curvature#prop:pet-ch3-curvature-symmetries","title":"prop:pet-ch3-curvature-symmetries","label":"prop:pet-ch3-curvature-symmetries"}],"backlinks":[{"kind":"uses","id":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch03_Curvature#def:pet-ch3-sectional-curvature","title":"def:pet-ch3-sectional-curvature","label":"def:pet-ch3-sectional-curvature"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch03_Curvature#prop:pet-ch3-jacobi-second-order-equation","title":"prop:pet-ch3-jacobi-second-order-equation","label":"prop:pet-ch3-jacobi-second-order-equation"}],"copy_context":{"version":1,"level":0,"node":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch03_Curvature#def:pet-ch3-directional-curvature-operator","title":"def:pet-ch3-directional-curvature-operator","tex":"\\begin{definition}[directional curvature operator]\n  \\label{def:pet-ch3-directional-curvature-operator}\n\n  \\source{petersen:page-0101}\n  \\uses{def:pet-ch3-curvature-tensor, prop:pet-ch3-curvature-symmetries}\n\n  For $v\\in T_pM$, the \\emph{directional} (or \\emph{tidal force}) \\emph{curvature\n  operator} is $R_v(w)=R(w,v)v:T_pM\\to T_pM$. It is self-adjoint and $v$ is always\n  a zero eigenvector, by the symmetry properties of $R$.\n\\end{definition}","source":"tex/references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch03_Curvature.tex","description":"","dependencies":[{"node":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch03_Curvature#def:pet-ch3-curvature-tensor","title":"def:pet-ch3-curvature-tensor","description":""},{"node":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch03_Curvature#prop:pet-ch3-curvature-symmetries","title":"prop:pet-ch3-curvature-symmetries","description":""}],"dependencies_omitted":0,"unresolved_dependencies":[],"unresolved_omitted":0}}